<p>After approx 15 seconds, a pair of trees at the edge of the visible field appear to stop moving as several more trees manifest through them and move past.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I <em>think</em> that what you're trying to convey here is that, due to the non-Euclidean nature of the space in SCP-3000, some of the trees appear to moving relative to an observer at rest, but the description is ambiguous due to the fact that you're also describing the movement of images across a video screen. That is, the images can be moving across a screen because they're moving relative to the ground, because the camera is moving relative to the ground, because the camera's view is rotating, or some combination of the above. Is a tree moving relative to the ground, with its roots moving <em>through</em> the soil as if the soil was water? If a tree and the soil it's in moving relative to the rest of the ground in the view? Are most of the trees moving across the screen because the camera is changing position, but one of the trees <em>isn't</em> moving across the screen because it's somehow keeping pace with the camera?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Trees that were formerly stationary at the edge of visibility continue moving forward.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, its unclear which trees are doing what.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>You've been walking through them for the past twenty minutes</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Past them, or literally through them?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Vector appears to apply not only to live observation, but also to video and audio recordings.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That sounds like it's antimemetic, not cognitohazardous.</p>
