<p>I'm incorporated some of the feedback you've provided, thank you. Some of it comes down to colloquial differences in language usage, but the changes I've made should clear it up.</p>
<p>Regarding cognitohazards, however, you're mistaken. Memetic hazards are specific to hazards which are perceived via some form of media, or are otherwise infectious from one mind to another. Cognitohazards can have any vector (visual, informational, auditory, tactile, or chemical), so long as the alterations to thought and perception are anomalous.</p>
<p>And with regards to antimemetics, the on-site material is written in such a way that antimemetics are not widely known or discussed throughout the rest of the Foundation, and this document was most recently updated by Dr. Marshall Grant, former HMCL of <a href="/scp-400">SCP-400</a>. He'll necessarily see it through that lens.</p>

